WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:08.500
In 1850, Helmholtz timed a nerve signal in a frog's leg.

2
00:00:09.440 --> 00:00:14.740
The signal ran at a few tens of metres per second, about the speed of a car on a

3
00:00:14.740 --> 00:00:18.460
motorway. So a nerve signal has a finite speed.

4
00:00:19.260 --> 00:00:22.620
What route does it take, from a sense organ to a muscle?

5
00:00:23.533 --> 00:00:27.233
The route is built from neurons, which are nerve cells.

6
00:00:28.013 --> 00:00:32.413
In a typical vertebrate motor neuron, the dendrites receive signals.

7
00:00:33.033 --> 00:00:35.133
The cell body gathers that input.

8
00:00:35.913 --> 00:00:39.053
A single long axon carries the signal away.

9
00:00:40.113 --> 00:00:43.433
Axon terminals at its far end pass the signal on.

10
00:00:44.253 --> 00:00:46.613
The signal on the axon is an impulse.

11
00:00:47.193 --> 00:00:51.653
It is all or none: it fires at full size, or not at all.

12
00:00:51.653 --> 00:00:58.953
At most synapses, the terminal releases a chemical that crosses a narrow gap to the next cell.

13
00:00:59.333 --> 00:01:04.173
A reflex is a rapid, automatic response to a stimulus.

14
00:01:04.933 --> 00:01:08.693
Its pathway is the reflex arc, which has five parts.

15
00:01:09.613 --> 00:01:11.533
A receptor detects the stimulus.

16
00:01:12.253 --> 00:01:15.453
A sensory neuron carries it into the central nervous system.

17
00:01:16.293 --> 00:01:18.073
An integrating centre sits there.

18
00:01:18.793 --> 00:01:21.033
A motor neuron carries the output out.

19
00:01:21.793 --> 00:01:24.653
The effector, a muscle or a gland, responds.

20
00:01:26.467 --> 00:01:28.667
Take the knee jerk in humans.

21
00:01:29.607 --> 00:01:33.947
A tap on the tendon below the kneecap stretches the quadriceps muscle.

22
00:01:35.147 --> 00:01:38.347
Stretch receptors there, the muscle spindles, fire.

23
00:01:39.407 --> 00:01:44.507
Their sensory fibres enter the spinal cord and synapse directly on the motor neurons

24
00:01:44.507 --> 00:01:45.967
of the same quadriceps.

25
00:01:46.807 --> 00:01:50.247
Those neurons make the muscle contract, and the leg kicks.

26
00:01:50.247 --> 00:01:56.267
This direct path has one synapse in the cord, so it is monosynaptic.

27
00:01:57.200 --> 00:02:01.320
Now the withdrawal reflex, pulling your foot away from a tack.

28
00:02:02.000 --> 00:02:04.580
It works through interneurons from the start.

29
00:02:05.540 --> 00:02:07.920
Pain receptors excite sensory neurons.

30
00:02:08.540 --> 00:02:11.100
These excite interneurons in the spinal cord.

31
00:02:11.900 --> 00:02:16.760
The interneurons activate motor neurons of the flexor muscles, and the limb lifts.

32
00:02:16.760 --> 00:02:21.120
Other interneurons inhibit the opposing extensor muscles.

33
00:02:22.033 --> 00:02:27.433
The knee jerk is a somatic reflex, because its effector is skeletal muscle.

34
00:02:28.253 --> 00:02:30.133
Somatic does not mean voluntary.

35
00:02:31.093 --> 00:02:37.293
Its direct path has one synapse, yet interneurons also quiet the opposing hamstring

36
00:02:37.293 --> 00:02:41.173
muscles. Many other reflexes use interneurons.

37
00:02:41.453 --> 00:02:42.633
So keep this rule.

38
00:02:42.633 --> 00:02:51.713
Trace any reflex as five parts: receptor, sensory neuron, integrating centre, motor

39
00:02:51.713 --> 00:02:53.433
neuron, and effector.
